    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1064DAE0FBE4FE97CBC8BAA9499D8EE7/jobs/347624": {
      "title": "Vice President for Financial Strategy & Resources",
      "description": "Vice President for Financial Strategy & Resources You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Vice President for Financial Strategy & Resources Maryville College - Maryville, TN 37804 Apply Apply Description MARYVILLE COLLEGE STAFF POSITION DESCRIPTION Position Title: Vice President for Financial Strategy & Resources Department: Business Office Title of Immediate Supervisor : President Funded Term / Work Cycle: 12 Months/Full-Time Expected Daily Work Hours: 8:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m. Expected Weekly Work Schedule: Monday – Friday; evening and weekend responsibilities as necessary to fulfill executive leadership responsibilities. Pay Grade : 16S Starting Salary Expectations :  $143,648 - $183,151, with starting salary determined based on relevant experience, qualifications, and demonstrated competencies. Application Instructions: Application materials including resumes and cover letters submitted by October 19, 2026 will be reviewed. About Maryville College: Maryville College is a nationally ranked institution of higher learning and one of America’s oldest colleges. For more than 200 years, we’ve educated students to be giving citizens and gifted leaders to study everything so that they are prepared for anything. Our graduates are equipped to address complex problems, engage with diverse communities, and launch meaningful careers. Nestled in Maryville, Tennessee, between the Great Smoky Mountains National Park and the city of Knoxville, our campus combines the beauty of a rural setting with the advantages of an urban center. With a wide range of academic programs and a strong tradition of career preparation, our alumni live “strong of mind and brave of heart,” carrying forward our Presbyterian founder’s charge to do good on the largest possible scale. Mission At Maryville College, we offer more than a mission—we offer support. Our employees receive a comprehensive benefits package including medical, dental, and vision coverage; a generous paid time off program; a retirement plan with an employer match of up to 5%; tuition benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents; and the opportunity to grow in a collaborative community that values professional excellence and personal well-being. “Maryville College prepares students for lives of citizenship and leadership as we challenge each one to search for truth, grow in wisdom, work for justice and dedicate a life of creativity and service to the peoples of the world.” Values: Scholarship: We commit ourselves to lifelong curiosity and learning, to the search for knowledge, and to intellectual creativity. Respect: We commit ourselves to honor the worth, dignity, and freedom of ourselves and all creation, and to treat others as we wish to be treated. Integrity: We commit ourselves to truth, honesty, dependability, and responsibility in all our actions and relationships. Position Purpose The Vice President for Financial Strategy & Resources serves as the College's chief financial officer and strategic advisor to the President, providing executive leadership for the institution's financial strategy, long-term fiscal sustainability, and stewardship of institutional resources. As a member of the President's Cabinet, the Vice President leads enterprise financial planning, resource allocation, capital strategy, and financial policy while partnering with the Board of Directors, Cabinet, and campus leaders to advance the College's mission and strategic priorities. Maryville is ultimately seeking a strategic, entrepreneurial, collaborative, and creative thinker and leader who balances risk-taking with pragmatism. The successful candidate will foster innovation, sound financial stewardship, and collaborative leadership, to position the College for long-term success. SUMMARY Reporting directly to the President, the Vice President for Financial Strategy & Resources provides strategic leadership for the College's financial enterprise, including long-range financial planning, investments, debt management, enterprise risk, capital planning, and institutional resource strategy. The Vice President serves as the President's principal advisor on financial matters and as liaison to the Board of Directors' Finance Committee, ensuring financial resources are aligned with the College's mission and strategic priorities. The position provides executive leadership for the Business Office and represents the College with financial institutions, governmental agencies, auditors, donors, and other external partners. Education and Experience required to ensure success in this position: Education Required Bachelor's degree in Finance, Accounting, Business Administration, Public Administration, Economics, Higher Education Administration, or a closely related field required. Master's degree in Business Administration (MBA), Finance, Accounting, Public Administration, Higher Education Administration, or related field required. Professional Certification Certified Public Accountant (CPA), Chartered Financial Analyst (CFA), Certified Management Accountant (CMA), or other relevant executive financial credential preferred. Experience Required Minimum of eight to ten (8 to 10) years of progressively responsible executive leadership experience in finance, business administration, or institutional operations. Demonstrated success leading strategic financial planning, multi-year forecasting, and institutional resource allocation. Experience advising executive leadership, governing boards, or comparable governing bodies. Demonstrated experience overseeing investments, debt management, banking relationships, capital planning, and enterprise financial strategy. Experience leading organizational transformation, strategic initiatives, and institutional change. Experience supervising mid-level leaders and building high-performing teams. Experience aligning financial planning with enrollment management, fundraising, and institutional strategic planning. Demonstrated success in presenting complex financial information to diverse audiences, including boards, executive leadership, faculty, and external stakeholders. Higher education leadership experience strongly preferred. Experience working within nonprofit organizations or mission-driven institutions preferred. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Leadership & Collaboration Serves as a member of the President's Cabinet, contributing to institutional leadership, strategic planning, and organizational decision-making. Advises the President and Board of Directors on financial strategy, institutional sustainability, enterprise risk, and resource stewardship. Provides executive leadership for the College's financial enterprise by establishing a shared vision that supports the mission and strategic priorities of the institution. Provides executive leadership for the Business Office and Bookstore. Jointly supervises Dining Services and Risk Management functions. Fosters a culture of collaboration, innovation, accountability, transparency, and continuous improvement across the Finance division. Builds and maintains collaborative partnerships with Cabinet members, faculty, staff, students, alumni, donors, community leaders, and external organizations. Develops, mentors, and retains high-performing leaders while promoting succession planning and professional development throughout the division. Represents the College in financial, governmental, professional, and community organizations as assigned by the President. Data, Technology & Compliance Establishes institutional financial policies, governance practices, and accountability measures that support effective decision-making and responsible stewardship. Ensures the College maintains appropriate financial controls, enterprise risk management practices, and compliance with applicable laws, regulations, accreditation standards, and Board policies. Provides executive oversight of financial reporting systems, business intelligence tools, and institutional financial analytics. Promotes the strategic use of technology and data analytics to improve operational effectiveness and institutional decision-making. Ensures financial reporting systems provide timely, accurate, and meaningful information for executive leadership and the Board of Directors. Oversees institutional banking relationships, investment management, debt compliance, insurance programs, and treasury functions. Serves as the College’s Risk Manager by overseeing plans to assess and mitigate risk, and protecting people, information and physical assets through appropriate communication, training and proactive relationships with third party providers. Stakeholder & Institutional Support Serves as the President's principal advisor on institutional financial matters. Acts as staff liaison to the Board of Directors’ Finance Committee and regularly presents financial reports, forecasts, and strategic recommendations. Collaborates with Cabinet leaders to align financial resources with institutional priorities, enrollment goals, academic initiatives, fundraising efforts, and operational objectives. Partners with Enrollment Management and Student Financial Services regarding tuition pricing, financial aid strategy, enrollment modeling, discount rate management, and net tuition revenue planning. Collaborates with Institutional Advancement to ensure responsible stewardship of restricted funds, endowed gifts, and scholarship resources. Builds productive relationships with auditors, financial institutions, rating agencies, governmental organizations, consultants, and external partners. Communicates complex financial information effectively to audiences with varying levels of financial expertise. Operations & Technical Oversight Provides executive oversight of Business Office operations. Oversees institutional accounting, audit, budgeting, grants administration, procurement, student financial services, investments, treasury management, and financial reporting through subordinate leadership. Ensures appropriate organizational structure, staffing, and operational effectiveness across the Finance division. Evaluates organizational performance and recommends improvements that strengthen operational efficiency, service delivery, and institutional effectiveness. Supports implementation of enterprise technologies and business process improvements that enhance financial operations and decision-making. Ensures appropriate coordination among Finance, Human Resources, Enrollment Management, Institutional Advancement, Information Technology, and other administrative divisions. Oversees all aspects of the College’s dining services (in conjunction with the VP/Dean of Students) and bookstore contracts. Fiscal Oversight Develops and advances the College's long-range financial strategy in support of institutional priorities and financial sustainability. Leads development of multi-year financial plans aligned with enrollment, fundraising, capital, and strategic planning initiatives. Provides executive oversight for institutional budgeting, financial forecasting, investments, debt management, cash management, capital financing, and enterprise resource allocation. Advises the President and Board regarding financial risks, opportunities, market conditions, and emerging trends affecting higher education. Evaluates financial performance, enrollment trends, demographic changes, and economic conditions to guide strategic decision-making. Develops innovative approaches to revenue diversification, resource optimization, and long-term financial sustainability. Oversees auxiliary enterprise financial performance and recommends strategies to strengthen institutional resources. Ensures responsible stewardship of College assets and financial resources. Strategic Planning & Sustainability Provides strategic leadership for the College's financial future through innovative planning and responsible fiscal stewardship. Partners with the President and Cabinet to develop and implement institutional strategic initiatives. Identifies opportunities to strengthen institutional resilience through new revenue streams, operational efficiencies, partnerships, and entrepreneurial initiatives. Develops financial strategies that support enrollment growth, academic excellence, student success, and institutional sustainability. Monitors higher education trends, financial markets, legislation, and economic conditions to anticipate future opportunities and challenges. Leads enterprise risk management initiatives that balance innovation with sound financial judgment. Promotes a culture of strategic thinking, creativity, collaboration, and continuous improvement throughout the institution. Ensures financial planning supports the College's mission while preserving flexibility to respond to future opportunities and challenges. Other: Performs other duties, responsibilities, and special projects as assigned to support the mission, strategic priorities, and operational effectiveness of Maryville College while contributing to a positive, respectful, and collaborative learning and working environment. NOTE: Other roles/duties will be assigned as necessary to assist the College in the attainment of the goals set forth and the enhancement of a positive, respectful learning environment for all staff, faculty, and students. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Comprehensive knowledge of higher education finance, strategic financial planning, capital planning, debt management, investments, enterprise risk management, and institutional budgeting. Demonstrated ability to develop innovative financial strategies that advance institutional priorities while ensuring long-term fiscal sustainability. Strong understanding of enrollment-driven financial models, tuition discounting, auxiliary operations, endowment management, and nonprofit finance. Exceptional executive leadership, organizational development, and talent management skills. Ability to analyze complex financial and organizational information and translate findings into strategic recommendations. Demonstrated ability to lead organizational change while fostering collaboration, trust, and accountability. Outstanding business acumen, negotiation, and relationship-building skills. Excellent written, verbal, and presentation skills with the ability to communicate complex financial concepts to audiences with varying levels of financial expertise. Demonstrated political acumen and emotional intelligence when working with governing boards, executive leadership, faculty, staff, donors, and community partners. Ability to build strategic partnerships that strengthen institutional effectiveness and financial sustainability. Commitment to innovation, continuous improvement, ethical leadership, and responsible stewardship of institutional resources. Demonstrated commitment to the mission of Maryville College and the value of a liberal arts education. License, Certification, or Registration Necessary: Certified Public Accountant (CPA), Chartered Financial Analyst (CFA), Certified Management Accountant (CMA), or other relevant executive financial credential preferred. Valid driver's license with the ability to travel locally, regionally, and nationally as required. Work Environment and Physical Requirements: Prolonged periods sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Ability to travel between campus buildings and off-campus locations as necessary. Ability to occasionally lift and/or move up to 25 pounds. Frequent attendance at evening and weekend meetings, Board of Directors meetings, College events, conferences, and community functions. Periodic regional and national travel representing the College with professional organizations, financial institutions, governmental agencies, and other external partners. Ability to operate the following vehicles or equipment: Standard office equipment including computers, printers, telephones, calculators, and presentation technology. College vehicle or personal vehicle for business travel as necessary. “The College may, at its discretion, sponsor employment-based immigration petitions (such as H-1B or permanent residency) on behalf of employees when permitted by law and aligned with institutional priorities and budget. Sponsorship is contingent on satisfactory continued employment, compliance with applicable federal regulations, availability of institutional resources, and the College’s sole determination of fiscal feasibility. The College cannot guarantee sponsorship in any case, particularly where newly introduced federal filing fees or wage obligations create financial burdens inconsistent with institutional budget stewardship. The College always reserves the right to defer or decline sponsorship.” Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1064DAE0FBE4FE97CBC8BAA9499D8EE7/jobs/343341": {
      "title": "Box Office & Guest Services Manager",
      "description": "Box Office & Guest Services Manager You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Box Office & Guest Services Manager Maryville College - Maryville, TN 37804 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description MARYVILLE COLLEGE STAFF POSITION DESCRIPTION Position Title: Box Office & Guest Services Manager Department: Clayton Center for the Arts Title of Immediate Supervisor: Executive Director, Clayton Center for the Arts Funded Term / Work Cycle: 12 Months Expected Daily Work Hours: 8 hours; schedule varies based on performances, rentals, and events Expected Weekly Work Schedule: Full-time; regular business hours with evening and weekend responsibilities as required Pay Grade : 7S Starting Salary Expectations :   $47,721.53 - $57,265.84, with starting salary determined based on relevant experience, qualifications, and demonstrated competencies. About Maryville College: Maryville College is a nationally ranked institution of higher learning and one of America’s oldest colleges. For more than 200 years, we’ve educated students to be giving citizens and gifted leaders to study everything so that they are prepared for anything. Our graduates are equipped to address complex problems, engage with diverse communities, and launch meaningful careers. Nestled in Maryville, Tennessee, between the Great Smoky Mountains National Park and the city of Knoxville, our campus combines the beauty of a rural setting with the advantages of an urban center. With a wide range of academic programs and a strong tradition of career preparation, our alumni live “strong of mind and brave of heart,” carrying forward our Presbyterian founder’s charge to do good on the largest possible scale. Mission At Maryville College, we offer more than a mission—we offer support. Our employees receive a comprehensive benefits package including medical, dental, and vision coverage; a generous paid time off program; a retirement plan with an employer match of up to 5%; tuition benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents; and the opportunity to grow in a collaborative community that values professional excellence and personal well-being. “Maryville College prepares students for lives of citizenship and leadership as we challenge each one to search for truth, grow in wisdom, work for justice and dedicate a life of creativity and service to the peoples of the world.” Values: Scholarship: We commit ourselves to lifelong curiosity and learning, to the search for knowledge, and to intellectual creativity. Respect: We commit ourselves to honor the worth, dignity, and freedom of ourselves and all creation, and to treat others as we wish to be treated. Integrity: We commit ourselves to truth, honesty, dependability, and responsibility in all our actions and relationships. Position Purpose The Box Office & Guest Services Manager leads box office, front-of-house, patron service, and volunteer usher operations for the Clayton Center for the Arts. The position ensures that performances, rentals, and special events are accurately built in ticketing and event-management systems and are appropriately staffed and prepared to deliver safe, welcoming, and professional patron and rental-client experiences. SUMMARY Reporting to the Executive Director, the Manager oversees box office and front-of-house operations; supervises the Guest Services Assistant(s); manages the volunteer usher program; coordinates security, bartending, concessions, and other front-of-house staffing; and serves as the primary ticketing and front-of-house connector for rental clients. The Manager works closely with Artistic Programming and Audience Development, Technical Operations, and administrative staff to ensure accurate event information, effective advance planning, and successful event execution. Education and Experience required to ensure success in this position: Education required to ensure success in this position: Bachelor's degree preferred; equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience will be considered. Experience required to ensure success in this position: Experience in box office operations, performing arts, venue or event management, hospitality, customer service, or a related environment required. Experience with ticketing and event-management software, front-of-house operations, volunteer coordination, concessions, security staffing, and employee supervision preferred. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Leadership and Collaboration: • Supervise, train, schedule, coach, and evaluate the Guest Services Assistant (s) and establish clear service and operational expectations. • Recruit, train, schedule, communicate with, and retain a reliable corps of volunteer ushers. • Coordinate part-time, student, temporary, contracted, and event-based personnel supporting front-of-house operations, including security, bartenders, concessions staff, and house-management coverage. • Collaborate with Artistic Programming and Audience Development, Technical Operations, administration, and rental clients to ensure coordinated event planning and execution. • Promote a culture of professionalism, hospitality, accountability, safety, and exceptional guest service. Data, Technology, and Compliance • Build and maintain performances, rentals, and ticketed events in the Center’s ticketing software, including event details, seating configurations, ticket prices, holds, sales schedules, and approved promotions. • Maintain accurate and timely front-of-house information in EMS, including ticketing, staffing, security, concessions, accessibility, house-management, and patron-service requirements. • Review upcoming events routinely to identify missing information, conflicts, staffing needs, or operational concerns before event day. • Maintain accurate ticketing, attendance, staffing, inventory, and volunteer records and provide operational reports as requested. • Ensure ticketing, concessions, alcohol service, patron safety, and front-of-house operations comply with College policies, contractual requirements, and applicable legal requirements. Stakeholder and Institutional Support • Serve as the primary ticketing and front-of-house contact for rental clients following confirmation of an event. • Work with rental clients to identify ticketing, seating, concessions, security, staffing, accessibility, merchandise, and other patron-facing requirements. • Communicate applicable policies, procedures, deadlines, staffing requirements, and costs to rental clients and confirm operational needs in advance. • Provide responsive and professional service to patrons, rental clients, volunteers, vendors, and other event stakeholders. • Resolve patron and client concerns appropriately and escalate significant issues when necessary. Operations and Technical Oversight • Manage daily box office operations, including ticket sales, exchanges, refunds, complimentary tickets, will-call, and patron inquiries in accordance with established policies. • Plan and oversee front-of-house operations for performances, rental events, and special events, including house opening, seating, late seating, intermissions, accessibility, concessions, and patron departure. • Determine front-of-house staffing requirements and coordinate security, bartending, concessions, ushers, and house-management coverage based on event needs. • Serve as House Manager or provide house-management coverage as needed and coordinate closely with Technical Operations regarding house opening, performance starts, intermissions, and event execution. Fiscal Oversight • Monitor expenses associated with front-of-house staffing, security, concessions, and patron services and assist with annual operating budgets and staffing projections. • Maintain appropriate concessions and beverage inventory levels and accurate records for purchasing, sales, and reconciliation. • Coordinate with Artistic Programming and Audience Development regarding approved ticket pricing, packages, promotions, group sales, and other ticketing initiatives. • Provide ticket sales, attendance, concessions, staffing, and other operational reports as requested and identify opportunities to improve efficiency and revenue performance. Strategic Planning and Sustainability • Establish and continually improve front-of-house, box office, volunteer, and patron-service procedures and standards. • Develop strategies to strengthen volunteer recruitment, engagement, recognition, reliability, and retention. • Recommend improvements to ticketing, EMS, concessions, staffing, and patron-service processes based on operational experience and data. • Support long-range planning for patron experience, accessibility, event staffing, and front-of-house operational needs. • Reduce preventable day-of-event issues through consistent advance planning, documentation, communication, and cross-departmental coordination. Other: • Perform other responsibilities and special projects as assigned to advance the College’s mission and maintain a respectful, collaborative learning environment. NOTE: Other roles/duties will be assigned as necessary to assist the College in the attainment of the goals set forth and the enhancement of a positive, respectful learning environment for all staff, faculty, and students. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • Exceptional attention to detail and ability to anticipate operational needs. • Strong commitment to hospitality and excellent patron and client service. . Familiarity with ticketing and event software as E-Tix and EMS. • Strong supervisory, volunteer-management, organizational, communication, and problem-solving skills. • Ability to translate event information into clear operational plans and remain calm and decisive in live-event environments. • Proficiency with ticketing, event-management, point-of-sale, CRM, or comparable technology; experience with EMS preferred. • Ability to manage multiple events, schedules, personnel, deadlines, and changing priorities simultaneously. License, Certification, or Registration Necessary: Valid driver’s license preferred for occasional business travel. Work Environment and Physical Requirements: • Prolonged periods sitting at a desk and working on a computer. • Frequent standing and walking throughout the facility during performances and events. • Ability to lift/move up to 25 pounds associated with concessions, event supplies, and front-of-house operations. • Ability to work evenings, weekends, and variable schedules associated with performances, rentals, and special events. • Ability to travel between campus buildings or off-campus locations as needed. Ability to operate the following vehicles or equipment: Standard office equipment, ticketing/POS equipment, and College or personal vehicle as authorized for business purposes. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1064DAE0FBE4FE97CBC8BAA9499D8EE7/jobs/342973": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Academic Advisor You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Academic Advisor Maryville College - Maryville, TN 37804 Apply Apply Description MARYVILLE COLLEGE STAFF POSITION DESCRIPTION Position Title : Academic Advisor Department: Academic Affairs Title of Immediate Supervisor: Director of Academic Advising Funded Term / Work Cycle: 12-Month; Full Time Expected Daily Work Hours: 8:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m. Expected Weekly Work Schedule: Monday – Friday Pay Grade: 6S Salary Expectation Range: $46,366 - $48,603 About Maryville College: Maryville College is a nationally ranked institution of higher learning and one of America’s oldest colleges. For more than 200 years, we’ve educated students to be giving citizens and gifted leaders to study everything so that they are prepared for anything. Our graduates are equipped to address complex problems, engage with diverse communities, and launch meaningful careers. Nestled in Maryville, Tennessee, between the Great Smoky Mountains National Park and the city of Knoxville, our campus combines the beauty of a rural setting with the advantages of an urban center. With a wide range of academic programs and a strong tradition of career preparation, our alumni live “strong of mind and brave of heart,” carrying forward our Presbyterian founder’s charge to do good on the largest possible scale. Mission At Maryville College, we offer more than a mission—we offer support. Our employees receive a comprehensive benefits package including medical, dental, and vision coverage; a generous paid time off program; a retirement plan with an employer match of up to 5%; tuition benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents; and the opportunity to grow in a collaborative community that values professional excellence and personal well-being. “Maryville College prepares students for lives of citizenship and leadership as we challenge each one to search for truth, grow in wisdom, work for justice and dedicate a life of creativity and service to the peoples of the world.” Values: Scholarship: We commit ourselves to lifelong curiosity and learning, to the search for knowledge, and to intellectual creativity. Respect: We commit ourselves to honor the worth, dignity, and freedom of ourselves and all creation, and to treat others as we wish to be treated. Integrity: We commit ourselves to truth, honesty, dependability, and responsibility in all our actions and relationships. Position Purpose The Academic Advisor, Title III Grant provides holistic academic advising and proactive student support designed to strengthen the first-year experience and improve student persistence. This position is funded through Maryville College’s five-year Title III Strengthening Institutions Program (SIP) grant, which supports initiatives that enhance academic success, improve the use of instructional technology, and expand data-informed approaches to student support. As part of this grant, the Academic Advisor helps implement a comprehensive student success model intended to improve academic performance, increase student engagement, and support the successful transition of first-year students. The Advisor provides individualized academic planning, outreach, and connections to campus resources, particularly for students who may experience academic or transitional challenges. This position supports new student onboarding and orientation, course selection, degree planning, and the use of academic support services. The Academic Advisor collaborates closely with the Title III Director, Director of Advising, faculty advisors, and campus partners to advance Title III project goals. This is a full-time, 12-month position, contingent upon continued annual federal funding. SUMMARY The Academic Advisor provides proactive academic advising and individualized student support to improve first-year academic success and persistence. This role assists students with academic planning, course selection, transition support, and connection to campus resources. The advisor uses data-informed outreach, maintains accurate documentation for Title III reporting, and collaborates with faculty and campus offices to ensure consistent, coordinated advising practices. Education and Experience required to ensure success in this position: Education required to ensure success in this position: Bachelor’s degree required Master’s degree preferred in advising, academic coaching, counseling, clinical psychology, social work, or a directly related liberal arts field from an accredited college or university Experience required to ensure success in this position: Minimum of one (1) year of experience providing academic advising & coaching and related student services for undergraduates, particularly for first-year students. Minimum of one (1) year of experience fostering student learning, support, and success for students at risk of attrition Minimum of one (1) year experience working with first-generation, and/or minority students. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Leadership & Collaboration Collaborate with the Title III Director to support grant-related advising and project goals Coordinate advising practices with the Director of Advising, faculty, and Student Affairs staff Participate in meetings, training sessions, and planning related to first-year support Data, Technology & Compliance Maintain accurate advising notes, records, and documentation in accordance with institutional policies and FERPA Use advising software, degree planning tools, and early-alert systems to monitor student progress Assist with Title III-required data tracking and reporting Stakeholder & Institutional Support Collaborate with the MC Cares team (Student Intervention Team) to monitor students’ academic and personal progress and intervene as necessary . Partner with faculty to address student academic concerns and clarify degree requirements Connect students with institutional resources such as tutoring, counseling, and academic support services Collaborate closely with the Director of Advising to support new-student registration process and in creating degree plans. Operations & Technical Oversight Provide academic planning guidance and course selection support Maintain advising schedules, communication plans, and follow-up protocols Support workshops or sessions that enhance academic skills or promote student success Fiscal Oversight No independent budget oversight Ensure advising activities and documentation align with Title III grant requirements and audit standards Strategic Planning & Sustainability Provide insights to improve advising processes and student success strategies Assist with documenting practices that support sustainability beyond the grant period Other: NOTE: Other roles/duties will be assigned as necessary to assist the College in the attainment of the goals set forth and the enhancement of a positive, respectful learning environment for all staff, faculty, and students. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Minimum of one (1) year of experience managing student academic files in accordance with recognized protocols, policies, and regulations applicable for FERPA, Title IX, etc. Knowledge of retention strategies and data-driven methods of identifying students who may be at risk of not continuing their education. Demonstrated understanding and a solid commitment to supporting students from diverse backgrounds Ability to work in partnership with staff, faculty, administrators, and student leaders. Understanding of liberal arts curricula and a commitment to helping students connect their liberal arts educations to their chosen career paths. License, Certification, or Registration Necessary: Valid Driver’s License Work Environment and Physical Requirements: WORK ENVIRONMENT Regular use of computers, phones, and standard office equipment Frequent interaction with students, faculty, staff, and campus support offices Occasional movement between campus buildings for meetings or student outreach Managing multiple priorities in a fast-paced setting Occasional evening or weekend hours during peak advising periods or student programming A balance of desk-based planning and high-engagement student interaction PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS Ability to remain in a stationary position for extended periods during design and development work Ability to move about campus as needed for consultations or training sessions Ability to lift and move up to 20 pounds (A/V equipment, training materials, or technology components) Sufficient manual dexterity to operate office technology and instructional design tools Ability to communicate effectively in person, by phone, and electronically Ability to operate the following vehicles or equipment: Computers and standard office equipment Student information systems, advising platforms, and retention tools Learning management systems for advising support Communication technologies including email, Teams, and messaging systems A/V equipment used for advising workshops or student presentations The College may, at its discretion, sponsor employment-based immigration petitions (such as H-1B or permanent residency) on behalf of employees when permitted by law and aligned with institutional priorities and budget. Sponsorship is contingent on satisfactory continued employment, compliance with applicable federal regulations, availability of institutional resources, and the College’s sole determination of fiscal feasibility. The College cannot guarantee sponsorship in any case, particularly where newly introduced federal filing fees or wage obligations create financial burdens inconsistent with institutional budget stewardship. The College always reserves the right to defer or decline sponsorship. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
